**The law: section 524B.** In 2022 Congress added section 524B to the US Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic (FD&C) Act; it took effect on **29 March 2023**. Per the FDA's [FAQ](https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/digital-health-center-excellence/cybersecurity-medical-devices-frequently-asked-questions-faqs), a **cyber device** is one that includes software, can connect to the internet, and has characteristics that could be vulnerable to cybersecurity threats. The FDA reads "connect" broadly: Wi-Fi, cellular, Bluetooth, other radio, inductive links, and even USB or serial ports count. Premarket submissions for cyber devices must include:

1. a plan to monitor, identify and address postmarket vulnerabilities, including coordinated disclosure;
2. processes giving reasonable assurance the device is cybersecure, and postmarket updates and patches;
3. an SBOM, including commercial, open-source and off-the-shelf components.
